WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.429
Film Courage: I’d love to hear your thoughts
on risk and failure?

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:06.930
Tosca Musk, Director, Producer and CoFounder
of Passionflix: Whoo!

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.920
Yeah, thoughts on risk and failure.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.850
I think both are vital.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:21.119
So I think you should continue to risk and
you will fail and when you do, you have to

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:26.640
get back up and you have to try again because
that’s the only way that you are going to

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.000
succeed.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.500
And true failure is when you don’t get back
up afterward.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.670
So failure is not continuing.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.300
Failure is giving up.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.350
So there is no option to fail.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:42.110
Film Courage: So with failure do you think
it becomes like a muscle, say almost like

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.960
stage fright, where you go to Toastmasters
or something and you get better at it.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.020
So with failure you’re maybe okay with it
more [often]?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.210
You’ve developed tougher skin?

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:56.310
Because I’ve seen it in reverse where failure
really guts people and they don’t get back

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.940
up.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:06.310
Tosca: Well, as I said, failure to me really
is not getting back up.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.210
So that is ultimate failure.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:16.450
Getting back up and continuing to do something
again, trying again, coming up with another

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.479
idea, another solution, is moving forward.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:24.609
And that is risky and it’s hard and painful
sometimes.

00:01:24.609 --> 00:01:29.179
But if you don’t get up and do it again,
then you’ve failed.

00:01:29.179 --> 00:01:35.990
So there are a lot of examples of people who
have gotten up and tried again.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:41.929
But most of the people who are successful
and have gotten up and tried again and continue

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:48.139
to try again and fail and if they fail again,
continue to try again and that means that

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:50.369
they haven’t failed.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:54.990
Film Courage: City of Los Angeles, you say
that a lot of people who come here are risk

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:55.990
takers.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.249
Do you feel that you were a risk taker when
you came?

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:01.779
Tosca: Did I say that people who come to Los
Angeles are risk takers?

00:02:01.779 --> 00:02:03.491
Film Courage: I don’t know?

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:05.450
I’m trying to see from my notes if you did.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.450
Maybe you did?

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.610
I don’t know if I just thought that?

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.599
Tosca: Well I think that if people are coming
to Los Angeles to be in the film industry

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:16.799
that is definitely taking a chance.

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:24.650
But you have a goal, you have a desire but
if you can remained focus which is very hard

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.819
especially in an industry that is filled with
rejection.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:35.299
Constant, constant rejection in every industry.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:42.440
But if you can continue in it, you will be
successful.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:48.790
You just have to stay focused and keep trying,
keep trying, keep trying, keep trying, and

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.489
ultimately you will get that job.

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:55.920
I mean you have to be good, so keep working
at your skill.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.450
Film Courage: Sure, sure.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.050
I think it’s difficult too because no one
usually gives you notes on why you didn’t

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.050
get it.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.042
I mean sometimes they do, but oftentimes they
don’t.

00:03:05.042 --> 00:03:08.340
And it’s all across the board for whatever
it is [in this industry].

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.080
So there are not script notes where someone
is saying “We like you but…”

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.630
You don’t know what it was.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.680
Tosca: And it is actually quite difficult
coming from the filmmaker’s standpoint (my

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.220
side of things) because I watch a lot of auditions
and we make decisions.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.219
And mother mother is also a model so I come
from that world where she constantly goes

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:28.489
through rejection and has for her entire life.

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:31.980
And while she’s a very successful model,
she is rejected continually.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.639
So I’ve been able to watch that and see
how she keeps going “Okay, next!

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:39.939
Okay, next!”

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:47.549
And then from my side of things (as the director)
“Well you might be good, but you don’t

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:48.549
look the part.”

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:50.620
And so I can’t hire you.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:59.739
So initially it’s all…it’s so subjective
and so if I then get an agent who comes back

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.540
to me and says “Can you give me feedback?”

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:06.520
Which many times they do, if I can give feedback
I will.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:12.930
But keep in mind if I audition 200 people,
I can’t give feedback for 200 people.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.000
I don’t have that kind of time.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.169
So it’s not that I didn’t like you.

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:22.860
Very likely, if you were in a room auditioning
for me, there is something about you that

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.210
I like.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.759
And so you’ve made it there, well done.

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:28.860
Congratulations to you.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.900
Keep going.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.400
You are in the room.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.710
So you made it there.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:38.400
That should be acknowledgment enough that
while you didn’t get the part, there were

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.180
200 people who were going for this same part
as you.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.650
And only one of you can get it.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.039
So 199 people will be rejected.

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:50.490
But 199 people still made it into the room,
I still recognize them.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.580
There are a couple of actors that I go every
single movie “Let’s see if we can put

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:55.580
them in this role?

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:56.580
On, no.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.580
It’s not going to work unfortunately.”

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.210
And so they are contacted constantly and told
“Hey maybe you will be in this role.”

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.210
And then they’re not.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:08.330
And it’s not anything other than it could
be timing, it could be, hair color, it could

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.810
be eye color.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.080
It could be the author wasn’t a big fan.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.080
It could be you’re too tall for the female
actor.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.870
Or too short for the female actor or you know
what I mean?

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.250
There are so many different aspects that go
into it that have nothing to do with your

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.690
ability to perform.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:30.309
So that’s why I say to actors keep going,
keep doing it!

